# Wiring Matinee to your existing model

`matinee.html` runs fully standalone right now — it has its own small sample
dataset and a JS similarity function, so it works with zero setup for demos
or a viva. Your notebook and CSVs aren't touched.

When you're ready to serve real recommendations from your trained model:

1. Save your fitted similarity matrix and movie list from the notebook
   (`pickle.dump(...)`) — this is the one new step, and it lives next to
   your existing `.ipynb`, not inside it.
2. Add a small `app.py` with Flask that loads those pickles and exposes
   one route:
   ```python
   @app.route("/recommend")
   def recommend():
       title = request.args.get("movie")
       # look up title, return top-5 similar titles + posters as JSON
       return jsonify(results)
   ```
3. In `matinee.html`, replace the body of `getRecommendations()` with a
   `fetch('/recommend?movie=' + encodeURIComponent(movie.title))` call and
   render whatever comes back. Everything else — layout, styling,
   selection state, animations — stays exactly as is.
4. Serve `matinee.html` from Flask's `templates/` (or `static/`) folder.

That keeps the frontend and the model cleanly separated: you can keep
improving the recommender in the notebook without ever touching the UI.
